     2	
     3	Written at the council's instruction after D544 (the work toward production goes on the new system; the old system stays the
     4	running default until the new one passes its gates). Every line that is not Alex's own is marked as proposed. Nothing here
     5	changes a gate, a line or a frozen piece.
     6	
     7	## 0. Frame
     8	
     9	- **Objective:** bring the new system (`clean-arch/`: Step 1 at configuration c8, Steps 2 and 3, the step that writes answer
    10	  choices, the frozen judge) through the gates below, so that it can replace the running default. Alex's word overrides the
    11	  choice and any gate at any time.
    12	- **Reference system:** the old system as it runs (`poc/config/poc-v118.json`), kept as the default and as the comparison.
    13	- **The ruler:** the frozen ruler stays as it is (D261); no further paid test on it (D482).
    14	- **Budget:** about $18.43 of D439's $40 recorded (Claude's ceiling, not Alex's). D544's first checkpoint: $5 of further
    15	  spending on this direction without a candidate that passes its prewritten small test returns the direction to the council;
    16	  $0.0022 of it used.
    17	- **Stops in force on the new system's pieces:** the step that writes answer choices is frozen as it ran in the fourth Stage A,
    18	  with no further tuning on Alex's 44 theses and no fifth run on them (D426); Steps 2 and 3 are frozen (D427); no more sentence
    19	  changes to Step 1 on the July articles (D473, D477); no theme or ladder change for the rental-price boundary (D477); the
    20	  judge is frozen (D354). A new version of any of these is a council decision under D439, reviewed before any send.
    21	
    22	## 1. The gates in force, in order (council round 7 of the cold thread)
    23	
    24	| Order | Gate | Its lines, and whose | Where the new system stands | What is not yet written |
    25	|---|---|---|---|---|
    26	| 1 | The step that writes answer choices (Stage A) | Alex's (D400): no question other than the topic's, no part left unlisted, no outcome with no range or two, no unjustified refusal; 35 of 44 topics right in two of three attempts, 28 of them by written choices; the same favourable end, and the same due day for nine in ten | **Not passed.** July, 40 fresh topics: the four hard lines each missed by one or two attempts even on the favourable count; 32 right, 22 by written choices (the council's scaled diagnostic, 32 and 26, is not Alex's number). Fresh week, 7 topics: no refusal; the omitted-clause marks of D547 | nothing |
    27	| 2 | The judge on the system's own questions (Stage B) | D428, against the baseline reading: unsupported amount answers at most five points above baseline; unsupported yes-or-no answers at most 5%; unsupported last answers at most 3 in 30, scaled; the design's completeness, coverage and control conditions | **Not passed** (2026-11-12): four qualifying controls with the needed material against eight required (question mismatches and missing material both contributed); the last-answer line missed on its strictest reading, 3 of 24 | nothing |
    28	| 3 | End to end against the old system, and Step 2's checks in that run | D427, D431: after gate 1 and 2, both systems unseeded on the frozen reference, the same day, three runs each; Step 2: search at least 90%, clear wrong merges at most 2%, no later detail lost through grouping | **Still owed as an acceptance.** The July census (new 2, 1, 0; old 1, 1, 1 of seven graded questions) was exploratory | a complete numerical definition of "beats" |
    29	| 4 | Use on unseen news | opened by Alex beside the existing gates (D484); it changes none of them by itself (D483). The lines in `ACCEPTANCE-DESIGN.md` (four in five big matters covered; at most one in five questions trivia or duplicate; nine in ten last answers supported; at most one in twenty cited statements wrong; no undelivered fact that would change an answer) are **proposed, not his** | **Not an accepted gate yet.** One week, one run, as evidence for its design: 2 of 5 matters (2 of 2 outside the financial lines D505 excludes), 1 of 7 trivia, 6 of 7 last answers supported by both reader families, three confirmed undelivered pairs | the lines; how D505 counts in coverage; the number of runs |
    30	| 5 | Integration with production storage and the pages, and release | the whole path integrated and accepted on the product's own path before it replaces the default | **Not started** | its acceptance and its numbers |
    31	
    32	## 2. The first blocker: worthwhile questions the step declines (gate 1, the count by written choices)
    33	
    34	The bounded question the council set: **can a general, permitted change recover a worthwhile question the system declines,
    35	without making it answer questions it should refuse?** Answered from the stored records alone
    36	(`clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/reachability/RESULT.md` with its theme census; `CHOICES-FOR-ALEX-2026-11-13.md`; the status
    37	page's record of D467 to D482).
    38	
    39	**The population.** In July the step's first pass declined eleven of the 40 topics. Two blind readers agree on ten: six are
    40	rightly declined (five matters; four of the six are topics the first step should not have opened: a completed stake, a stake
    41	too small to matter, a background sentence, an outcome no public material settles; two are the survey question Alex ruled out,
    42	D417); four are worth a question the article supports, on two matters. One is split. Reaching the scaled 26 needs all four and
    43	no loss elsewhere.
    44	
    45	| Lead | The mechanism on record | A general change? | What stands against it |
    46	|---|---|---|---|
    47	| the memory-price pair | the first step's shape of the topic: a rolling twelve months; a second figure (a bank's estimate of one rack's memory cost) that no public material settles | none on record that is permitted: two first-step candidates aimed at this class failed their lines (c15yc, c17), first-step sentence changes on these articles are stopped (D473, D477), and the answer-choice step declines a rolling window by Alex's own ruling (D417) | the stops themselves |
    48	| the rental-price pair (one article) | the step's rule "a matter that no theme in the request's themes covers", applied to twelve themes shown by id and name only. The census: the same topic declined in 5 of 9 attempts and given choices in 4 (three under "Semiconductor cycle", one under another theme); memory prices go under "Semiconductor cycle" in every attempt, though that theme's stored watch list holds only industry revenue growth. Six topics in all were declined for coverage in 12 attempts, three of them given choices in other attempts | in principle: tell the step what each theme carries, so that coverage is decided against a definition and not a name | what a theme carries is a product definition in `poc/universe.json`, which the old system shares; its stored definition of that theme does not include prices, so showing it as it is would make the step decline prices more consistently, not less; the definition itself is the first of the three choices written for Alex on 2026-11-13, untested. D477 stopped a theme or ladder change for this boundary after the check that the question, where it was opened, was decisive in no judgement of that window. The Stage A readers had ruled these refusals right on the list as it stands |
    49	
    50	**Upside and protection.** The rental-price pair is at most 2 of the 4 the scaled count needs (22 to 24 of 26). None of the six
    51	right refusals is a refusal for theme coverage, so a change of what the themes carry would not by itself turn them into
    52	questions; the three other topics declined for coverage (a lidar ban, the CPU-to-GPU ratio in inference, Huawei's chip output)
    53	have never been read for whether they are worth a question.
    54	
    55	**Answer: no candidate.** The records establish a mechanism for one of the two matters, and for that one the change is a
    56	product definition already put to Alex and stopped by a council decision, worth at most half the gap, with nothing on record
    57	that the recovered question would receive sound choices or matter to a judgement. For the other matter no permitted change
    58	exists. On the stored evidence the count by written choices is not reachable by a permitted change to the answer-choice step:
    59	its distance is set upstream, by the topics the first step opens (rightly refused ones, and the memory shapes).
    60	
