# My own read of the 30 sends, written before any reader's row (2026-11-16)

Every A2 send was read beside the control's sends of the same topic, against the topic's description and outcome. These are my
leads; the readers' marks and the third readers decide the counts.

## Line 2 (a part left unlisted)

- **f1/clean-14 (China accelerator units, a figure):** in all 6 sends the question tracks total units. Every send lists the GPU versus
  non-GPU split and IDC's two forecasts. I find no unlisted part in either arm.
- **f1/clean-15 (hyperscaler custom silicon in gigawatts, a figure):** in all 6 sends the question tracks custom-silicon gigawatts.
  - Control p1 lists only the Nvidia comparison. The outcome's "resulting split of their accelerator spending" is left unlisted,
    and so is Maia's role as the primary inference system: the control's likely L failure.
  - Control p2 lists the comparison and the spending split. Maia's role is neither in its question nor in its list: a possible L
    failure.
  - Control p3 puts Maia in the question and lists the rest.
  - A2 lists the comparison, the spending split and Maia's role in all three sends (p2 as "context").
  - This is the one topic where A2 lists more than the control, in the direction A2 was built for.
- **f1/clean-17 (Taiwan's export controls, an event):** both arms list mirroring U.S. rules and the effects on Nvidia. Control p1
  puts coverage of Nvidia's products into the question. The other five sends list coverage. I find no unlisted part in either arm.
- **f1/clean-18 (the Poolside licence, a figure):** all 6 sends list the amounts paid and any change of terms. I find no unlisted
  part in either arm.
- **Lead:** the control fails L on at most 1 topic (clean-15), so by the registered rule even population (a) would read
  "inconclusive". On these fresh topics the frozen step already lists most of the outcome's named parts. The development topics'
  rate (the control failing L on 6 of 13) does not show here, on a sample of 4 written topics.

## Refusals (line 4)

- **f1/clean-16 (the Sanders–Casar AI bill):** all 6 sends decline. The reason is the same in both arms: U.S. AI legislation is not a
  listed theme and not a question about NVDA.
- This is the step's rule, not A2's change: the arms do not differ here.
- Whether a pause on advanced AI development is "about NVDA" (it would cut its demand) is the readers' call (N).

## Over-listing (H), A2's known harm

- I see no entry in either arm that a rung already decides.
- **A2 clean-15 p1** lists "the share of total capacity on custom silicon". That is a derived figure, a borderline part.
- **A2 clean-17 p2 and p3** list coverage of Nvidia's products as a scope matter, as the control does.

## Correction, written after the readers' first rows (same day): the line-6 lead below is wrong

The table below reads the model's raw `due_day`. The product and line 6 read the stored view's due day, which code sets (D414;
`accounting.py` line 6 reads `o['view']['due_day']`).

| Topic | Due day shown, both arms, every send |
|---|---|
| clean-14 | 2027-03-31 |
| clean-15 | 2028-03-30 |
| clean-17 | 2027-01-31 |
| clean-18 | 2027-04-01 |

So all 4 written topics keep one due day across their sends in both arms. Line 6 is unmeasured here in any case: it needs 20 topics
with three written sends. The raw guess moves, but code overrides it, so it is no defect. Kept as a record of my own misread: I read
the raw field, not the stored field the product uses.

## (Superseded) Not one of A2's lines, but in view: line 6 (the same due day across the three sends)

| Topic | Control due days | A2 due days |
|---|---|---|
| clean-14 | 03-31, 02-28, 04-13 | 03-31, 06-30, 06-30 |
| clean-15 | 02-28, 03-31, 03-31 | 03-31 ×3 |
| clean-17 | 01-31 ×3 | 01-31 ×3 |
| clean-18 | 02-28, 02-24, 02-28 | 02-28, 02-28, 02-24 |

- Every due day that moves has the basis "expected_publication": the step guesses a publication date, and the guess moves from send
  to send.
- **Control:** 1 of 4 written topics has the same due day in all three sends. **A2:** 2 of 4.
- Line 6 needs 9 in 10. Both arms miss it on fresh material, and A2's change does not touch it. This is a production finding for
  the gate, apart from A2.
